const faq = [
  {
    q: 'Should I take a secured or unsecured education loan for study abroad in India?',
    a: 'Take a secured loan if your family can offer property, fixed deposits, or another acceptable asset as collateral and does not mind the paperwork and valuation time — it typically comes with a lower interest rate and a higher loan ceiling. Take an unsecured loan if you have no collateral to offer, need faster sanction, or are targeting a university that qualifies for a public-sector bank\'s collateral-free list. The trade-off is a rate that usually runs 1-2 percentage points higher and a lower maximum loan amount at most lenders.',
  },
  {
    q: 'Can I get an education loan for study abroad without collateral?',
    a: 'Yes. Public-sector banks offer limited collateral-free loans, usually capped around Rs 40-50 lakh, only for applicants admitted to specific premier institutions on the bank\'s approved list. NBFCs such as Avanse and HDFC Credila offer larger unsecured loans, sometimes beyond Rs 75 lakh to over Rs 1 crore, for students admitted to a wider set of recognised universities, based on the co-applicant\'s income and the course\'s employability. International lenders like Prodigy Finance and MPOWER Financing lend without any collateral or Indian co-signer at all, underwriting the loan mainly on your admitted program and projected future income.',
  },
  {
    q: 'Which lender is best for a study abroad education loan: bank, NBFC, or international lender?',
    a: 'There is no single best option — it depends on your collateral, timeline, and target university. Public-sector banks usually offer the lowest interest rates but slower processing and stricter university lists. NBFCs process faster, cover a wider range of universities, and offer larger unsecured amounts, at a moderately higher rate. International lenders like Prodigy Finance and MPOWER skip Indian collateral and co-signers entirely but usually charge the highest rates and only fund a narrower list of eligible programs and schools. Many students use an NBFC or international lender to secure the visa-stage funding proof quickly, then refinance into a lower-rate bank loan later if the numbers work out.',
  },
  {
    q: 'What is a moratorium period on a study abroad education loan?',
    a: 'The moratorium period is the stretch during your course plus a set grace period after it ends, typically six months to a year depending on the lender, during which you are not required to repay the principal. Some lenders demand nothing at all during this window; others require simple interest payments during the course itself, which keeps the total interest burden from compounding as steeply once full repayment begins. Confirm exactly which version your lender offers before you compare two loans on rate alone, because a full moratorium and an interest-only moratorium change your real total repayment cost significantly.',
  },
  {
    q: 'Does my university choice affect whether I can get an education loan?',
    a: 'Yes, directly. Every collateral-free or reduced-collateral loan product is tied to a specific list of eligible universities and, often, eligible courses within them, ranked by factors like global rankings, past placement data, and the lender\'s own risk models. A university or program outside that list may still qualify for a loan, but usually only with full collateral and a more conservative loan amount. Check your target university against a lender\'s eligible list before you finalise admission offers, not after, since this can change which lender is even willing to fund your specific program.',
