---

<BaseLayout
  title="Education Loan for Study Abroad India: Secured vs Unsecured, Bank vs NBFC"
  description="Education loan for study abroad India compared: secured vs unsecured options, public-sector bank vs NBFC vs international lenders, moratorium rules, and how your university choice decides who will fund you."
  canonical={canonical}
  ogType="article"
  ogImage={ogImage}
  jsonLd={jsonLd}
>
  <Nav activePage="blog" />

  <article id="edu-loan-abroad-article">
    <header class="hero">
      <div class="hero-bg">
        <div class="orb orb-gold"></div>
        <div class="orb orb-teal"></div>
        <div class="hero-grid"></div>
      </div>

      <div class="container hero-inner">
        <nav class="breadcrumb" aria-label="Breadcrumb">
          <a href="/">Home</a>
          <span aria-hidden="true">&rsaquo;</span>
          <a href="/blog">Blog</a>
          <span aria-hidden="true">&rsaquo;</span>
          <a href="/blog/study-abroad">Study Abroad</a>
          <span aria-hidden="true">&rsaquo;</span>
          <span aria-current="page">Education Loan for Study Abroad India</span>
        </nav>

        <div class="hero-meta">
          <span class="pill hero-pill"><span class="pill-dot"></span>Students and families funding a degree abroad</span>
          <span class="date-pill">Published: {publishedDateLabel}</span>
          <a class="date-pill date-pill--link" href={authorUrl}>By {authorName}</a>
        </div>

        <div class="hero-grid-main">
          <div class="hero-copy">
            <h1>Education loan for study abroad India: secured vs unsecured, and who actually lends</h1>
            <p class="line strong">
              An education loan for study abroad India comes in two structurally different forms — secured, where your family pledges property or another asset for a lower rate, and unsecured, where you pay more but risk nothing tangible. Which one you can even get, and from whom, depends less on your credit history and more on three things: whether your family has collateral to offer, whether your target university sits on a lender's approved list, and how fast you need the money before a visa deadline. This article breaks down banks, NBFCs, and international lenders side by side, so you shop by real terms instead of the first offer your admissions consultant hands you.
            </p>

            <div class="key-takeaways">
              <p class="key-takeaways__label">The short version</p>
              <ul>
                <li><strong>The default answer should be no to a large study-abroad loan unless the exact program is top-tier for the subject or creates another scarce, verified career advantage.</strong></li>
                <li>Public-sector secured loans are usually cheapest but need collateral and take longer; NBFC and international options can be faster or unsecured but normally cost more and may cover fewer institutions.</li>
                <li>Check the lender's eligible university and course list, then compare the total repayment cost across the full moratorium and tenure—not just the headline interest rate.</li>
                <li>Model visa failure, six to twelve months without the expected job, exchange-rate movement, and repayment from an India-level salary before signing.</li>
                <li>Use roughly 10%-25% of the family's total education budget as a conservative planning range where possible. It is a debt-avoidance heuristic, not a universal financial rule; exceeding it needs unusually strong evidence and adequate family security.</li>
              </ul>
            </div>

            <p class="line">Worried a low headline rate is hiding a stricter university list you don't qualify for?</p>
            <p class="line">Confused about whether pledging your parents' property is worth the lower rate?</p>
            <p class="line">Not sure if an NBFC that processes in a week is a shortcut or a trap?</p>
            <p class="line">Stop comparing rates alone — compare structure first.</p>
            <p class="line">This article shows you exactly what changes between a bank, an NBFC, and an international lender.</p>

            <div class="cta-pair">
              <a class="btn-gold pulse" href={guidanceUrl}>Get personalised career guidance</a>
              <a class="btn-outline cta-assessment-lite" href={assessmentsUrl}>Free career and skill assessments</a>
            </div>
          </div>

          <aside class="hero-board card">
            <div class="board-kicker">The fast answer</div>
            <div class="board-row">
              <span>1</span>
              <p>Check whether your family has usable collateral before you pick a lender type.</p>
            </div>
            <div class="board-row">
              <span>2</span>
              <p>Confirm your exact university and course sit on the lender's eligible list.</p>
            </div>
            <div class="board-row">
              <span>3</span>
              <p>Read the moratorium terms — full moratorium and interest-only are not the same cost.</p>
            </div>
            <div class="board-row">
              <span>4</span>
              <p>Price the total repayment, not just the interest rate shown on the homepage.</p>
            </div>
          </aside>
        </div>
      </div>
    </header>

    <section class="container section">
      <h2>Why the lender type matters more than the rate you see first</h2>
      <p class="line">
        Most students shopping for an education loan for study abroad India start by comparing interest rates across a few homepages. That comparison is close to useless on its own, because the rate a lender advertises usually applies to a specific product tier — a secured loan, a top university, or a high-income co-applicant — that may not match your actual situation. The real first question is structural: are you offering collateral or not, and does your target university even qualify for the loan type you want.
      </p>
      <p class="line">
        If you are still deciding whether the degree itself is worth the loan, read
        <a href="/blog/study-abroad/is-ms-in-usa-worth-it-india/">the real ROI math on an MS abroad</a> before you shop for funding. If you have already decided and want help matching a loan structure to your specific finances and risk comfort,
        <a href={guidanceUrl}>talk through the numbers with a counsellor</a> before you sign a sanction letter.
      </p>
      <p class="line">
        This article covers four things in order: how secured and unsecured loans actually differ, how public-sector banks, NBFCs, and international lenders compare on rate, speed, and eligibility, how the moratorium period changes your real repayment cost, and how your course and university choice decides which lenders will even consider your application.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section class="container section">
      <h2>Secured vs unsecured education loan for study abroad: the real trade-off</h2>
      <p class="line">
        A secured education loan requires your family to pledge an asset — usually residential or commercial property, but sometimes fixed deposits, government bonds, or other approved securities — as collateral against the loan. An unsecured loan requires no such pledge, and the lender instead underwrites the loan against your admitted course, the co-applicant's income, and the university's placement track record.
      </p>

      <div class="grid-two">
        <div class="card">
          <h3>Secured loan</h3>
          <ul>
            <li>Lower interest rate, typically the cheapest option a bank or NBFC offers.</li>
            <li>Higher loan ceilings, often covering the full cost of an expensive program.</li>
            <li>Requires property, FDs, or another acceptable asset with clear title, plus valuation and legal paperwork.</li>
            <li>Processing usually takes longer because of property valuation and mortgage documentation.</li>
            <li>Family collateral is legally at risk if the loan defaults.</li>
          </ul>
        </div>
        <div class="card">
          <h3>Unsecured loan</h3>
          <ul>
            <li>No property or asset pledge required — approval rests on course, university, and co-applicant income.</li>
            <li>Faster sanction, often within one to two weeks at NBFCs, useful against visa deadlines.</li>
            <li>Interest rate typically runs 1-2 percentage points higher than a secured loan from the same lender.</li>
            <li>Loan ceilings are usually lower unless you qualify for a top-tier university's collateral-free list.</li>
            <li>Family financial exposure is capped at the loan liability, not tied to a specific asset.</li>
          </ul>
        </div>
      </div>

      <p class="line">
        For loans above Rs 7.5 lakh at public-sector banks, the standard model requires a parent as joint borrower and tangible collateral security regardless of whether the loan is otherwise marketed as reduced-collateral, along with an assignment of the student's future income toward instalments. NBFCs and some private banks structure this differently, offering fully unsecured products at higher amounts for students admitted to their approved university list.
      </p>

      <div class="honest-take card">
        <p class="honest-label">Honest take</p>
        <p class="line">
          A lower rate on a secured loan is only a real saving if your family can genuinely absorb the risk of losing that asset in a worst-case scenario. Do not pledge a family's only home for a 1-2 percentage point rate difference without running the actual rupee gap over the full tenure and weighing it against what happens if repayment gets difficult in year one or two after graduation.
        </p>
      </div>
    </section>

    <section class="container section">
      <h2>Public-sector banks vs NBFCs vs international lenders</h2>
      <p class="line">
        Three broad lender categories fund study abroad education in India, and each one solves a different problem for a different kind of applicant.
      </p>

      <div class="card">
        <div class="table-shell">
          <table>
            <thead>
              <tr>
                <th data-label="Lender type">Lender type</th>
                <th data-label="Typical rate range">Typical rate range</th>
                <th data-label="Collateral needed">Collateral needed</th>
                <th data-label="Speed">Processing speed</th>
              </tr>
            </thead>
            <tbody>
              <tr>
                <td data-label="Lender type">Public-sector banks (SBI Global Ed-Vantage and similar)</td>
                <td data-label="Typical rate range">Roughly 8.4%-9.4% p.a., generally lowest for secured loans</td>
                <td data-label="Collateral needed">Required above Rs 7.5 lakh in most cases; limited collateral-free tier for premier-ranked universities up to roughly Rs 40-50 lakh</td>
                <td data-label="Speed">Slower — property valuation and documentation typically take several weeks</td>
              </tr>
              <tr>
                <td data-label="Lender type">Private banks (HDFC Credila and similar)</td>
                <td data-label="Typical rate range">Roughly 8.9%-10.75% p.a. depending on secured or unsecured</td>
                <td data-label="Collateral needed">Secured option available for lower rates; unsecured available up to roughly Rs 75-80 lakh for eligible universities</td>
                <td data-label="Speed">Faster than public banks — often 1-2 weeks for sanction</td>
              </tr>
              <tr>
                <td data-label="Lender type">NBFCs (Avanse and similar)</td>
                <td data-label="Typical rate range">Roughly 11%-14% p.a.</td>
                <td data-label="Collateral needed">Unsecured loans up to roughly Rs 1-1.2 crore for eligible institutions and courses</td>
                <td data-label="Speed">Fast — built around quick sanction for visa-stage documentation</td>
              </tr>
              <tr>
                <td data-label="Lender type">International lenders (Prodigy Finance, MPOWER Financing)</td>
                <td data-label="Typical rate range">Generally the highest of the four categories</td>
                <td data-label="Collateral needed">No collateral and no Indian co-signer required at all</td>
                <td data-label="Speed">Fast, but limited to a narrower list of eligible programs, mostly graduate degrees at specific partner schools</td>
              </tr>
            </tbody>
          </table>
        </div>
      </div>

      <p class="line">
        Prodigy Finance underwrites almost entirely on your admitted program and projected future income, which is why it can skip collateral and a co-signer altogether — but it currently funds only master's-level programs, and only at partner universities on its list. MPOWER Financing works similarly for undergraduate, graduate, and PhD programs, but only at its own list of roughly 500 partner universities, concentrated in the US and Canada. Neither is a universal option; both are built for students who lack Indian collateral and are admitted to a school on their respective lists.
      </p>

      <div class="honest-take card">
        <p class="honest-label">Honest take</p>
        <p class="line">
          A common and reasonable pattern is to take the fastest available unsecured loan, from an NBFC or an international lender, to lock in visa-stage funding proof against a tight admission deadline, then refinance into a lower-rate secured bank loan once you have time to complete the collateral paperwork. Since prepayment and foreclosure charges on floating-rate loans to individual borrowers were banned by RBI direction from January 2026, refinancing later carries no penalty on most current-generation loans — confirm this with your specific lender before assuming it applies to your sanction letter.
        </p>
      </div>
    </section>

    <section class="container section">
      <h2>How the moratorium period actually works</h2>
      <p class="line">
        The moratorium period is the window during your course, plus a grace period after it ends, when you are not required to repay the loan's principal. Nearly every education loan for study abroad India includes one, but the fine print differs enough between lenders to change your real total cost significantly.
      </p>

      <div class="grid-two">
        <div class="card">
          <h3>Full moratorium</h3>
          <p class="line">Some lenders, including Prodigy Finance, require no payment at all — neither principal nor interest — during the course and a grace period of around six months after it ends. This keeps monthly cash flow easiest during study, but unpaid interest typically accrues and gets added to the principal, increasing what you eventually repay.</p>
        </div>
        <div class="card">
          <h3>Interest-only moratorium</h3>
          <p class="line">Other lenders, including MPOWER Financing and many Indian banks and NBFCs, require simple interest payments during the study period itself, with principal repayment starting only after the grace period ends. This adds a real monthly cost while you are still studying, but it prevents interest from compounding into the principal, which usually lowers the total amount repaid over the full loan tenure.</p>
        </div>
      </div>

      <p class="line">
        Neither structure is automatically better — it depends on whether you or your family can manage interest-only payments during the course years. What matters is that you do not compare two loan offers on interest rate alone without checking which moratorium structure each one uses, since a lower headline rate with full-moratorium interest accrual can end up costing more in total than a slightly higher rate with an interest-only moratorium.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section class="container section">
      <h2>How your university and course choice decides who lends to you</h2>
      <p class="line">
        This is the part most students discover too late: collateral-free and reduced-rate loan products are not available to every admitted student — they are tied to a specific list of eligible universities, and sometimes eligible courses within those universities, set by each lender.
      </p>
      <p class="line">
        Public-sector banks typically restrict their collateral-free tier to a defined set of premier institutions, often filtered by global rankings or the bank's own past placement data. NBFCs maintain their own, usually broader, lists based on course employability and historical repayment performance from graduates of that specific program. International lenders like Prodigy Finance and MPOWER go further — they only fund programs at their own partner-school lists, and outside those lists, they generally will not lend at all, regardless of your profile.
      </p>

      <div class="honest-take card">
        <p class="honest-label">Honest take</p>
        <p class="line">
          Check your shortlisted universities against a lender's eligible-institution list before you finalise your admission choices, not after you have already paid a deposit. A university that looks equally strong on paper can put you in a completely different funding bracket — full collateral required at one lender, collateral-free at another — purely because of how that lender's approved list is built.
        </p>
      </div>

      <div class="cta-pair">
        <a class="btn-gold pulse" href={guidanceUrl}>Get personalised career guidance</a>
        <a class="btn-outline cta-assessment-lite" href={assessmentsUrl}>Free career and skill assessments</a>
      </div>
    </section>

    <section class="container section">
      <h2>Protecting your ability to repay: do not fund the degree alone</h2>
      <p class="line">
        A large education loan is only a good decision if it is genuinely funding a stronger earning outcome — and that outcome is never guaranteed the day you land. Job markets shift between the year you take the loan and the year you graduate, so build repayment safety into the plan itself, not just into the loan structure.
      </p>

      <div class="grid-two">
        <div class="card">
          <h3>Verify the salary evidence before you borrow</h3>
          <p class="line">Check your specific program's recent placement data, typical starting salary range, and how that number is described — median, strong outcome, or a small number of exceptional cases — rather than relying on a single headline salary figure from a university brochure or agent.</p>
        </div>
        <div class="card">
          <h3>Keep a backup skill and income path live</h3>
          <p class="line">Build a second, more portable skill alongside your main degree, and keep your professional network and job search active in both your target country and India, so a slow visa or hiring market in one place does not leave you with no income and a full moratorium clock running out.</p>
        </div>
      </div>

      <p class="line">
        This matters more the higher and more prestige-driven the loan amount is. A large loan for a highly selective, expensive program is a reasonable bet when there is real, current evidence — verified placement outcomes, licensing or accreditation requirements, or lab and research access you cannot get elsewhere — behind the number, not general university reputation alone. <strong>Treat the loan as funding a high-income skill portfolio you are actively building, not just a credential you are purchasing.</strong>
      </p>
    </section>

    <section class="container section">
      <h2>A quick decision filter before you apply for a loan</h2>
      <p class="line">
        Use this as a starting filter, then verify current rates, eligible-institution lists, and moratorium terms directly with each lender before you commit.
      </p>

      <div class="card">
        <div class="table-shell">
          <table>
            <thead>
              <tr>
                <th data-label="Your situation">Your situation</th>
                <th data-label="Leaning">Leans toward</th>
              </tr>
            </thead>
            <tbody>
              <tr>
                <td data-label="Your situation">Family has property or FDs to pledge and time before your visa deadline</td>
                <td data-label="Leaning">Secured loan through a public-sector bank for the lowest rate</td>
              </tr>
              <tr>
                <td data-label="Your situation">No collateral available, but your university is on an NBFC's eligible list</td>
                <td data-label="Leaning">Unsecured NBFC or private-bank loan for faster sanction</td>
              </tr>
              <tr>
                <td data-label="Your situation">No collateral, no Indian co-signer, admitted to a partner-listed university</td>
                <td data-label="Leaning">International lender such as Prodigy Finance or MPOWER Financing</td>
              </tr>
              <tr>
                <td data-label="Your situation">Tight visa deadline but you would prefer a lower long-term rate</td>
                <td data-label="Leaning">Take the fastest unsecured option now, refinance to a secured bank loan later</td>
              </tr>
              <tr>
                <td data-label="Your situation">Uncertain your family can manage interest-only payments during the course</td>
                <td data-label="Leaning">Prioritise a full-moratorium lender, but budget for the larger principal it creates</td>
              </tr>
            </tbody>
          </table>
        </div>
      </div>
    </section>

